        "Note",
        '<strong>B’reshit (<em>בְּרֵאשִׁית</em>).</strong> The opening word names the entire book in Hebrew tradition. Grammatically a construct ("in the beginning of…") rather than absolute, leading some translators (Rashi, NRSV margin) to render "When God began to create" — turning v.1 into a temporal clause introducing v.2, with v.3 as the main action. The translation choice has theological consequences for the doctrine of <em>creatio ex nihilo</em>.',
