        "Ephrem the Syrian (362).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 362 AD)</small><p>On &#x27;the earth was without form, and void; and darkness was upon the face of the deep. And the Spirit of God moved upon the face of the waters&#x27; Ephrem reads the threefold negation (tōhū wā-bōhū + darkness + deep) as the proper unfolded condition of created matter awaiting form. Against any reading that would treat the primal chaos as evil or co-eternal, Ephrem insists matter is created good but unformed; form is the divine speech-act that makes the unformed habitable. The Spirit&#x27;s &#x27;moving&#x27; (the Syriac merahhefa carries the sense of a bird brooding over its nest) Ephrem reads as the active warmth that prepares matter for the Word&#x27;s subsequent commands. This pneumatological-cosmological reading is the Syriac tradition&#x27;s most distinctive contribution to early creation theology.</p></aside>',
