        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Tanninim (<em>תַּנִינִים</em>) — sea-dragons.</strong> The Hebrew word renders elsewhere as “dragon” (Isa 27:1, Ezek 32:2), and is the same word used of Leviathan and Rahab. The LXX has <em>kētē</em> (κήτη), “great sea-monsters,” the same word used in the Greek Jonah for the swallowing fish. The Genesis 1 verse subtly demythologizes the chaos-monster tradition: in Mesopotamian myth Marduk slays Tiamat and her brood; here, God simply creates them and pronounces them good.",
        "User paraphrase; references LXX",
