        "very good",
        "comm-rabbinic",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The culminating verdict.</strong> The phrase <em>tov meʾod</em> (“very good”) closes the creation week with a verdict stronger than the prior six “good”s. Most commentators, both Jewish (Rashi) and Christian (Aquinas, <em>ST</em> Ia.65.2), read this as an ontological affirmation of created reality — neither evil nor neutral but actively good. The phrase becomes foundational for theological positions resisting Manichaean and Gnostic dualism, which would identify matter or the material world with evil. The sevenfold <em>tov</em> structure is itself a literary achievement — six “good”s plus one “very good.”",
        "User paraphrase; references Rashi, Aquinas",
