        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (362).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 362 AD)</small><p>&#x27;And God saw every thing that He had made, and, behold, it was very good&#x27; Ephrem reads as the consummative divine judgment on the entire six-day creative work. &#x27;Very good&#x27; (tab tab — the Syriac doubles the adjective for intensity) Ephrem takes as ontological assessment, not merely aesthetic: the cosmos as a whole, in its differentiated unity, perfectly fulfills the divine intention. Against any later Marcionite or gnostic reading that would treat matter or embodiment as defective, Ephrem insists Gen 1:31 is the theological foundation for the goodness of creation — including matter, body, sexuality, work, and time — all &#x27;very good&#x27; in their proper places. The fall in Gen 3 corrupts but does not negate this primordial goodness.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Genesis I.31 (NPNF S2 V13). PD.",
