        "Yahweh God",
        "word",
        "Hebrew",
        "Yahweh Elohim — the compound name",
        "<strong>Yahweh Elohim.</strong> Genesis 2:4b is where the personal divine name <em>YHWH</em> first appears in the Bible, and it appears in compound with <em>Elohim</em> — the more abstract or generic word for God used throughout Gen 1. The compound (occurring 20 times in Gen 2–3, then almost never again in the Pentateuch as a unit) signals theological identity: the cosmic Creator of chapter 1 (<em>Elohim</em>) is the same as the covenant God who breathes life into Adam (<em>YHWH</em>). The Targums and rabbinic tradition heard in this compound an early theological assertion of unity between the universal Creator and the particular God of Israel.",
        "User original",
