        "Ephrem the Syrian (360).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 360 AD)</small><p>On &#x27;God breathed into his nostrils the breath of life&#x27;, Ephrem reads the breath (nefesh / nešmā) not as a separable substance but as the divine in-spiration that gives Adam his unique standing among creatures. The Ethiopian Andəmta commentary on this verse stresses that the breath is what makes the human a fitting addressee for revelation — not merely a living animal but a hearer of the Word.</p></aside>',
