        "A river went out of Eden",
        "parallel",
        "Parallel",
        "Parallel: Ezek 47:1–12 / Ps 46:4 / Rev 22:1",
        '<strong>The river of Eden across the canon.</strong> The river that "goes out of Eden to water the garden" reappears in three crucial later texts: <a href="index_split_046.html#ch-b43-c47">Ezekiel 47:1–12</a> sees a river flowing from the temple, making the sea fresh; <a href="index_split_033.html#ch-b30-c46">Psalm 46:4</a> celebrates "a river whose streams make glad the city of God"; and <a href="index_split_060.html#ch-b86-c22">Revelation 22:1</a> completes the pattern with "the river of the water of life… flowing from the throne of God and of the Lamb." Eden\'s river becomes temple-river becomes new-creation-river.',
