        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (360).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 360 AD)</small><p>Ephrem&#x27;s reading of the serpent emphasizes deception by speech rather than ontological evil: the serpent is a creature whose form Satan borrows for the purpose of the temptation. Tewahedo tradition, drawing on the Synaxarium accounts of the Watchers (cf. 1 Enoch 6-11), connects this fallen-angel-acting-through-a-beast pattern to the wider angelology of the Ethiopian canon.</p></aside>',
