        "in his own likeness, after his image",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Image transmitted through Adam.</strong> The image-and-likeness language of 1:26–27 is here applied to Seth as Adam’s son — pointedly NOT to Cain (5:3 follows the Cain genealogy of ch 4 and reorients to Seth). Some commentators read this as deliberate exclusion: the divine image transmits through Seth’s line, not Cain’s. The Hebrew syntax (in his own likeness, after his image) inverts the order of 1:26 (in our image, after our likeness) — Adam transmits in reverse what God bestowed. The <em>Imago Dei</em> thus carries not only theological but genealogical implications: the line through which it passes becomes the line of promise (Seth → Noah → Abraham → David).",
