        "comm-modern-critical",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Antediluvian longevity.</strong> The pre-flood patriarchs’ lifespans (Adam 930, Methuselah 969, Jared 962) parallel the Sumerian King List, which records eight to ten antediluvian kings reigning 18,600 to 43,200 years each. Both traditions distinguish a primordial age of extraordinary longevity from the historical present. Three interpretive options dominate modern scholarship: literal historical (lifespans gradually shortened by environmental or divine factors); symbolic-numerological (the ages encode mathematical or sexagesimal patterns); or genealogical-compressive (figures represent lineages, not individuals). Sailhamer emphasizes that all the patriarchs nevertheless died, against Eden’s apparent promise — death’s universality is the chapter’s main point.",
        "User paraphrase; references Sumerian; summarises Sailhamer",
