        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (362).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 362 AD)</small><p>Lamech&#x27;s naming of Noah — &#x27;This same shall comfort us (yenahamenu) concerning our work and toil of our hands, because of the ground which the LORD hath cursed&#x27; — Ephrem reads as prophetic anticipation grounded in pre-flood corruption. Noah&#x27;s name (the Hebrew root n-w-h, to rest/comfort) Ephrem takes as etymologically programmatic: Noah will be the one through whom the post-flood relief is mediated, the cursed ground (Gen 3:17) being either washed clean or replaced by new earth in the renewal that follows the flood. Ephrem reads Lamech&#x27;s hope as proleptically Christological: every Old Testament expectation of comfort-mediated-by-a-righteous-one ultimately points beyond itself to Christ as the true Comforter. The Tewahedo Synaxarium preserves Noah on the 10th of Magabit as a type of Christ.</p></aside>',
