    (
        6,
        1,
        "",
        "When men began to multiply",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Multiplication as crisis",
        "<strong>Multiplication becomes crisis.</strong> The blessing of 1:28 (“be fruitful, multiply, fill the earth”) returns here as the trigger for catastrophe — humanity multiplies, and with multiplication comes the wickedness that leads to the flood. The narrative gives a structural irony: the very fertility that was God’s first blessing now produces the conditions for destruction. The same verb <em>rabah</em> (“multiply”) frames both 1:28 and 6:1, deliberately. Genesis 6 is the inversion of Genesis 1.",
        "User original",
    ),
