        "",
        "blameless among the people of his time",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Righteous in his generations.</strong> Famous rabbinic dispute (b. Sanhedrin 108a; <em>Bereshit Rabbah</em> 30:9): was Noah righteous absolutely, or only relative to his corrupt generation? Two readings: (1) defending Noah — even in evil times he was righteous, so he would have been more so in better times; (2) qualifying Noah — only by comparison to his generation, undistinguished if measured against Abraham. The phrase <em>be-doroṯav</em> (“in his generations”) is the textual hinge. The dispute is theologically generative: it questions whether moral standing is absolute or contextual.",
        "User original",
