        "flood of waters",
        "word",
        "Hebrew",
        "mabbul — the technical flood-term",
        '<strong><em>Mabbul</em> (מַבּוּל) — the great flood.</strong> A rare technical term used almost exclusively for the Noahic deluge (Gen 6–11; Ps 29:10). Etymologically debated: traditionally connected with <em>nabal</em> (“to fall”), giving “the falling [of waters]”; an alternative connects it with Akkadian <em>bubbulu</em> (a yearly inundation). Psalm 29:10 uniquely places God “enthroned over the <em>mabbul</em>” — using flood-imagery to depict divine sovereignty over chaotic waters, picking up the Genesis 1:2 Tehom motif. The LXX renders it <em>kataklysmos</em>, which the NT uses for the flood (<a href="index_split_052.html#ch-b60-c24">Matthew 24:38</a>; 2 Peter 2:5).',
        "User paraphrase; references LXX",
