    (
        6,
        22,
        "p",
        "Thus Noah did. He did all that God commanded him",
        "parallel",
        "Parallel",
        "Noah’s faith in Hebrews 11",
        '<strong>Noah’s faith.</strong> <a href="index_split_059.html#ch-b78-c11">Hebrews 11:7</a> takes precisely this verse as the proof-text of Noah’s faith: “By faith Noah, being warned about things not yet seen, with godly fear prepared an ark for the saving of his household, by which he condemned the world, and became heir of the righteousness which is according to faith.” The NT writer reads the obedience formula (“He did all that God commanded”) as the outward sign of inward trust in unseen warnings — a paradigm for Christian eschatological faith.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        7,
        1,
        "",
        "Come with all of your household into the ship",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Household salvation.</strong> The motif of a righteous person’s whole household saved on his account recurs through Scripture: Lot’s family (Gen 19), Rahab’s house (Josh 6:25), the jailer at Philippi (“you and your household will be saved” — Acts 16:31). Hebrews 11:7 specifically commemorates Noah’s faith as the means of his household’s preservation. The household-as-religious-unit pattern is foundational for biblical conceptions of corporate solidarity and informs the New Testament practice of household baptism (Acts 16:33; 1 Cor 1:16).",
        "User original",
    ),
