        "p",
        "second month, on the seventeenth day",
        "parallel",
        "Parallel",
        "Flood as baptismal type",
        '<strong>The flood as baptismal type.</strong> <a href="index_split_059.html#ch-b80-c3">1 Peter 3:20–21</a> reads the flood typologically: “in the days of Noah … wherein few, that is, eight souls, were saved through water. This is a symbol of baptism, which now saves you.” Peter takes the ark/water relationship as a figure of Christian baptism — the same waters that destroy the disobedient bear up the obedient. Patristic interpretation extends this: Tertullian (<em>De Baptismo</em> 8) treats Noah’s dove as the type of the Spirit at Christ’s baptism (Matt 3:16), creating a creation→flood→baptism trajectory.',
        "User paraphrase; references Tertullian",
