        "Only Noah was left",
        "parallel",
        "Parallel",
        "Days of Noah / Days of the Son of Man",
        '<strong>“As in the days of Noah.”</strong> Both <a href="index_split_052.html#ch-b60-c24">Matthew 24:37–39</a> and <a href="index_split_054.html#ch-b62-c17">Luke 17:26–27</a> set the flood as the typological pattern for the unexpected coming of the Son of Man: people were “eating, drinking, marrying, being given in marriage, until the day Noah entered the ark” — and the flood came suddenly. The contrast is between the obliviousness of the many and the readiness of the few. <a href="index_split_059.html#ch-b81-c3">2 Peter 3:5–7</a> escalates the typology: just as the world “then existing was destroyed by water,” so the present heavens and earth “are reserved for fire” at the day of judgment.',
