    (
        8,
        1,
        "p",
        "God remembered Noah",
        "word",
        "Hebrew",
        "zakar — covenantal remembering",
        "<strong><em>Zakar</em> (זָכַר) — covenantal remembering.</strong> God’s “remembering” is not the recovery of forgotten data but the activation of covenant care. The verb appears at every covenantal pivot: God remembers Abraham at Lot’s rescue (19:29), remembers Rachel (30:22), remembers his covenant with the patriarchs at the Exodus (Ex 2:24; 6:5), remembers his covenant with David (Ps 132:1). When God “remembers,” he acts. The pivot of the flood narrative is exactly this verb: the de-creation phase ends, the new-creation phase begins, with <em>vayyizkor Elohim</em>. Compare the Magnificat: “he has helped his servant Israel, in remembrance of his mercy” (Luke 1:54).",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        8,
        2,
        "",
        "The deep’s fountains and the sky’s windows were also stopped",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Re-creation: Day 2 restored",
        "<strong>The cosmos re-created.</strong> If 7:11 was the un-doing of Day 2 (firmament separation), 8:2 is its re-doing. The waters above and below are again separated, the firmament re-established. The next verses re-enact the rest of creation in mirror order: dry land emerges (8:3–14, mirroring Day 3); birds and animals come out (8:17, mirroring Days 5–6); humanity is blessed “be fruitful and multiply” (9:1, restating 1:28); food provisions are spelled out (9:3, expanding 1:29). The Noah narrative is structurally a re-creation account — the world starts over.",
        "User original",
    ),
