        "",
        "flesh with its life, that is, its blood, you shall not eat",
        "comm-orthodox",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The blood prohibition.</strong> The first dietary law in Scripture, predating Sinai. The prohibition on eating blood becomes foundational for kashrut (Lev 17:10–14) and is explicitly retained in the apostolic decree at Jerusalem for Gentile Christians (Acts 15:20, 29). The theological reasoning: blood is life (<em>nephesh</em>), and life belongs to God. Eating blood would be a usurpation of divine prerogative over life itself. Some Christian traditions (Eastern Orthodox, Ethiopian) have continued to observe the blood prohibition; most Western Christianity has not, treating it as part of ceremonial law.",
        "User original",
