    (
        9,
        6,
        "",
        "Whoever sheds man’s blood, his blood will be shed by man",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Capital punishment grounded in imago Dei",
        '<strong>Capital punishment and the image of God.</strong> The first explicit lex talionis is grounded not in social order or vengeance but in the image of God: to murder a human is to attack a divine icon. The structure is chiastic in Hebrew (<em>shofek dam ha-adam ba-adam damo yishafek</em>) — the symmetry is itself the argument. Note what the verse does NOT say: nothing about the state, courts, or due process; the imago Dei foundation is what later Christian and Jewish ethics build the legal apparatus upon. <a href="index_split_051.html#ch-b60-c5">Matthew 5:21–22</a> deepens the principle: the prohibition extends inward to the murderous heart. <a href="index_split_059.html#ch-b79-c3">James 3:9</a> applies the imago Dei foundation to verbal cursing.',
        "User original",
    ),
