        '<strong>The Noahic foundation of human dignity.</strong> The first explicit prohibition of murder grounds itself in the <em>imago Dei</em>. This verse becomes foundational in later Jewish jurisprudence (Mishnah Sanhedrin 4:5: "whoever destroys a single soul, scripture imputes it to him as if he had destroyed an entire world") and in Christian natural-law arguments for human rights from Aquinas to the Universal Declaration. Notice the chiastic structure: <em>shofeḥ dam ha’adam, ba’adam damo yishafeḥ</em> — "the spiller of human blood, by the human his blood shall be spilled."',
        "User paraphrase; references Mishnah, Aquinas",
