        "never again be a flood to destroy the earth",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The Noahic covenant.</strong> The first explicit covenant (<em>berit</em>) language formally enacted. The Noahide covenant is unilateral on God’s part — no human stipulations are required, only divine commitment. It is also universal — “with you and your offspring and every living creature.” This contrasts sharply with the later Mosaic covenant (bilateral, particular to Israel) and prepares the conceptual space for the Pauline “new covenant” (universal, unilaterally divine in initiative). The covenant’s permanence (“never again”) is repeated three times in this chapter for emphasis.",
        "User original",
