        "",
        "",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The curse of Canaan — a passage with troubled history.</strong> Noah curses Canaan (son of Ham) rather than Ham himself, and the reason for the curse is itself contested. This passage was wrongly used through the medieval and modern eras to justify the enslavement of African peoples (the ‘curse of Ham’ doctrine), an interpretation universally rejected by contemporary scholarship as eisegesis with no textual basis.",
        "User original",
