        "p",
        "Let him dwell in the tents of Shem",
        "parallel",
        "Parallel",
        "Japheth in the tents of Shem",
        '<strong>Japheth in the tents of Shem.</strong> The blessing on Japheth — “may God enlarge (<em>yapht</em>, wordplay on Yapheth) Japheth, and let him dwell in the tents of Shem” — has been read by Christian tradition since at least the patristic period as a prophecy of the inclusion of the Gentiles (Japhethite peoples) in the worship and salvation that comes through Shem (Israel, the line of Christ). <a href="index_split_056.html#ch-b65-c11">Romans 11:17–24</a> explicitly grafts Gentile branches into the Israelite olive tree; <a href="index_split_058.html#ch-b69-c2">Ephesians 2:13</a> declares Gentiles “brought near by the blood of Christ.” Justin Martyr (<em>Dialogue with Trypho</em> 139) and Augustine read 9:27 as the architectural blueprint of Christian Gentile inclusion.',
        "User paraphrase; references Augustine",
