        "comm-rabbinic",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Lifnē Yahweh — “before Yahweh.”</strong> The phrase is ambiguous: it can mean “with God’s favor” or “in God’s face” (i.e., defiantly). The Targum Pseudo-Jonathan and most rabbinic sources take the second meaning, casting Nimrod as God’s opponent and the architect of Babel. Josephus (<em>Antiquities</em> 1.4) makes him the first tyrant. Augustine (<em>City of God</em> 16.4) reads the proverb as preserving the memory of his God-defying audacity. The text itself is studiedly ambivalent.",
        "User paraphrase; references Targum, Pseudo-Jonathan, Josephus, et al.",
