        "the father of all the children of Eber",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Eber and “Hebrew.”</strong> Eber (<em>ʿēḇer</em>, <em>עֵבֶר</em>) is the eponymous ancestor of the Hebrews (<em>ʿiḇrim</em>) — though the linguistic relationship is debated. Some scholars derive “Hebrew” from “Eber”; others from a verbal root meaning “to cross over” (the Habiru of second-millennium BCE texts, displaced or marginal peoples). Either etymology connects Israel’s identity to a wider Semitic context, distinguishing Hebrew identity from Canaanite identity (Eber’s line is Shemitic, distinct from Ham’s Canaanite line). The note positions Israel proleptically within the Table of Nations.",
        "User original",
