        "Elam, Asshur, Arpachshad, Lud, and Aram",
        "comm-contextual",
        "Note",
        "Shemite peoples — Mesopotamian Semites",
        "<strong>Shemite peoples.</strong> The five sons of Shem: <em>Elam</em> = Elamites (southwestern Iran; non-Semitic linguistically, but politically grouped with Mesopotamian Semites); <em>Asshur</em> = Assyrians; <em>Arpachshad</em> = the line that yields Eber, Peleg, and ultimately Abram (the chosen line; the genealogy in 11:10–26 picks up here); <em>Lud</em> = Lydia (Anatolia) — a Semite among Indo-Europeans, anomalous; <em>Aram</em> = Arameans (Syria, ancestors of the Aramaic language family). The grouping centers Mesopotamia and the Levantine east. Note that the Eber → Hebrew (<em>‘ivri</em>) etymology surfaces here: Israel is the chosen line within the Shemite cluster.",
