        "they are one people, and they all have one language",
        "comm-patristic",
        "Note",
        "The unbounded power of unified humanity",
        '<strong>The unbounded power of unified humanity.</strong> God’s words — “nothing will be withheld from them which they intend to do” — are not anxiety but diagnosis: the unification of language and purpose places humanity on a trajectory of unlimited collective power, unchecked by moral or material constraint. Augustine (<em>City of God</em> 16.4) reads the verse as exposing the totalizing ambition of the earthly city; modernity reads it as a remarkably accurate prediction of technological civilization. The dispersal that follows is presented as mercy as much as judgment — fragmented humanity is constrained from the projects that united humanity could undertake. <a href="index_split_055.html#ch-b64-c2">Acts 2:6</a> reverses the curse not by re-unifying language but by giving the gift of mutual understanding across diversity.',
        "User paraphrase; references Augustine",
