        "Come, let’s go down",
        "comm-reformation",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Divine plural.</strong> The “us”/“let us” plural recurs in Genesis (1:26 “in our image”; 3:22 “like one of us”; here at Babel). Three traditional readings: (1) plural of majesty (Targums); (2) divine address to angelic council (Calvin, modern critical scholarship); (3) Trinitarian (Augustine, classical Christian theology). The Babel context complicates option 3 — it would make the Trinity actively dispersing humanity. Most modern scholars favor (2). The plural underscores Babel as a counter-action: humanity speaks “Come, let us…” (11:4) and God responds “Come, let us…”",
