        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The oak of Moreh.</strong> A specific terebinth tree (Hebrew <em>elon</em>, <em>אֵלוֹן</em>) at Shechem becomes one of the patriarchal landmarks. Trees served as religious markers in ANE Canaanite cult; Abraham’s adoption of the site re-consecrates a previously sacred location. The name “Moreh” (<em>מוֹרֶה</em>, “teacher” or “oracle”) suggests the tree was an oracular site. The same tree (or its successor) reappears in Joshua 24:26 (Joshua’s covenant ceremony) and Judges 9:6 — locating Israel’s covenant memory in this geographic axis.",
        "User original",
