        "that you are my sister",
        "comm-patristic",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The wife-sister motif.</strong> Three patriarchal narratives feature the wife-as-sister deception: Abraham in Egypt (Gen 12), Abraham in Gerar (Gen 20), Isaac in Gerar (Gen 26). Source-critical scholarship treats these as variants of a single tradition; canonical readers see them as escalating moral examples. The deception is technically true — Sarah is Abraham’s half-sister (Gen 20:12) — but the omission of “wife” creates lethal danger. Patristic discomfort at the morally compromised patriarch produced extensive justifying commentary (Augustine, Chrysostom).",
        "User paraphrase; references Augustine, Chrysostom",
