        "very rich in livestock, in silver, and in gold",
        "comm-contextual",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Patriarchal wealth.</strong> Abraham’s wealth — described in tripartite form (livestock, silver, gold) — is theologically loaded. It establishes Abraham as a powerful nomadic chieftain, aligning with archaeological evidence for second-millennium BCE Semitic chieftains in the Levant. The specific mention of silver and gold (not just livestock) marks Abraham as a participant in the international trade economy of the ancient Near East. The wealth is also implicitly a divine blessing — promised in 12:2 and now realized through the Egypt episode.",
