        13,
        8,
        "",
        "we are relatives",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Abram’s magnanimity.</strong> The chapter stages one of the OT’s clearest pictures of patriarchal generosity: Abram, the elder kinsman, has every customary right to first choice of the land but offers Lot the choice instead. The Hebrew <em>anashim aḥim</em> ("we are kinsmen") establishes the theological-relational frame — clan unity outweighs territorial advantage. The contrast with Lot’s subsequent choice (13:10–11, eyes on the well-watered Jordan plain) makes Abram’s self-restraint look retrospectively prudent: Lot ends up in Sodom, while Abram receives unconditional promise of all the land (13:14–17). The narrative encodes one of the patriarchal cycle’s recurring patterns: those who grasp lose, those who release receive. The same theology will return in Jacob’s eventual restoration of Esau’s blessing (33:9–11), in Joseph’s magnanimity to his brothers (50:20), and ultimately in the prophetic critique of land-grasping (Isa 5:8). Abram here is the inaugural model.',
        "User original",
