        13,
        10,
        "",
        "like the garden of Yahweh",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Lot’s fateful choice.</strong> The narrator’s aside — "like the garden of Yahweh, like the land of Egypt" — is dense with foreshadowing. The comparison to Eden ("the garden of Yahweh") prepares the reader for what follows: a paradise-region that will become the OT’s archetype of catastrophic destruction. The comparison to Egypt is equally pointed: Lot is choosing land like the place his uncle has just escaped (12:10–20), with the same agricultural lushness that makes empires powerful and morally precarious. The narrator’s "before Yahweh destroyed Sodom and Gomorrah" is also chronologically precise: 13:10 is post-destruction perspective inserted into the moment of Lot’s choice, alerting the reader that the landscape Lot is admiring is already, from the narrator’s vantage, ruined. The literary technique is one of Genesis’s most sophisticated — proleptic narrator-comment that reframes a present scene with future knowledge. Lot is choosing what looks like Eden but is in fact future ash.',
        "User original",
