        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Sodom’s wickedness foregrounded.</strong> The narrator gives an editorial preview of Sodom’s moral state — preparing the reader for the destruction in chapter 19. The phrase “exceedingly wicked and sinners against Yahweh” (<em>raʿim ve-chataʾim</em>) doubles the moral indictment. Ezekiel 16:49–50 specifies Sodom’s sins as pride, gluttony, ease, and refusal to help the poor — broader than the sexual reading often dominant in Christian tradition. The narrative placement here, just as Lot moves toward Sodom, creates dramatic foreshadowing.",
