        "the oaks of Mamre",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The oaks of Mamre.</strong> Mamre (in Hebron) becomes Abraham’s primary residence and the site of the most intimate theophany in Genesis — chapter 18’s three-visitor scene, eventually identified with Yahweh. The terebinths/oaks (Hebrew <em>elonei</em>) of Mamre persisted as a pilgrimage site through the Roman period — Constantine built a basilica there, the ruins of which are visible today. The tree-altar pattern of patriarchal religion (cf. 12:6 Moreh, 21:33 Beersheba tamarisk) marked sacred trees as theophanic landmarks.",
        "User original",
