    (
        15,
        18,
        "",
        "from the river of Egypt to the great river, the river Euphrates",
        "comm-contextual",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The territorial promise.</strong> The verse defines the Abrahamic land-grant in maximal geographical terms: from the Wadi of Egypt (the river system on the Sinai border, not the Nile itself) to the Euphrates. The territory described is wider than any historical Israelite kingdom ever controlled — even at the height of David and Solomon, the empire reached the Euphrates only briefly through tributary kingdoms. The verse’s ideological function is theologically programmatic: the divine promise exceeds any historical realization, leaving the unfulfilled portion as eschatological surplus. Modern political readings of "biblical Israel’s borders" sometimes appeal to this verse and to Num 34, both of which describe the territorial promise rather than any historical-archaeological reality. The chapter’s preceding ritual — the cutting in two of animals through which Yahweh alone passes (15:10, 17) — is one of the OT’s most unusual covenant ceremonies, transferring all the curse-implications of covenant breach to the deity rather than the human partner. The verse is the ratification of a covenant Yahweh has unilaterally guaranteed.',
        "User original",
    ),
