        "",
        "Sarah laughed within herself",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Sarah’s laughter.</strong> Sarah’s laughter parallels Abraham’s at 17:17, but receives divine rebuke (18:13) where his did not. The rebuke is indirect — God asks Abraham why she laughed, indicting her through her husband. Some commentators distinguish posture (Abraham fell on his face; Sarah laughed inwardly without external reverence); others read the asymmetry as patriarchal narrative bias. Hebrews 11:11 rehabilitates Sarah, citing her faith. The threefold-laughter pattern (Abraham, Sarah, the future Isaac whose name means “he laughs”) makes laughter the covenant’s core motif.",
        "User original",
