        "the Judge of all the earth do right",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Judge of all the earth.</strong> Abraham’s question to God is the Bible’s first great theodicy — and audaciously, Abraham instructs God in justice. The verse establishes that divine justice is not merely whatever God does, but accountable to a moral standard that is itself divine. Jewish tradition (especially Levinas, <em>Totality and Infinity</em>) reads this as the founding moment of ethical theism — Abraham bargaining with God on behalf of strangers. The verse has been foundational for theodicies from Job to the modern question of innocent suffering.",
        "User original",
