        32,
        "",
        "I will not destroy it for the ten",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Ten righteous.</strong> Abraham bargains God down from fifty righteous to ten. The number ten becomes minimum-quorum in rabbinic Judaism — a <em>minyan</em> requires ten adult Jews for communal prayer, drawing on this verse. The implication for Sodom is devastating: not even ten righteous could be found. Modern theology has read the bargaining as the founding pattern of Abrahamic intercession — the prayer that argues with God on behalf of others. The pattern recurs in Moses (Ex 32:11–14) and culminates in Christ’s intercession for the world.",
        "User original",
