        1,
        "",
        "Lot sat in the gate of Sodom",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Lot in the gate.</strong> Sitting “in the gate” (<em>be-shaʿar</em>) was the Hebrew idiom for civic authority — gates were where elders rendered judgment, transactions were witnessed, and public matters resolved (Deut 21:19; Ruth 4:1–11). Lot has thus become an established figure in Sodom’s civic life — no longer the sojourner but a member of the city’s leadership. The detail makes his subsequent rejection by the townspeople (“This one fellow came in to live as a foreigner, and he appoints himself a judge!” 19:9) more poignant — they reject the very integration Lot had claimed.",
        "User original",
