    (
        19,
        8,
        "",
        "two virgin daughters",
        "comm-patristic",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Lot’s morally compromised offer.</strong> Lot’s offer of his virgin daughters is one of the most disturbing texts in Genesis — and the narrative neither praises nor explicitly condemns it. Ancient interpretation has been deeply uncomfortable: rabbinic tradition (<em>Bereshit Rabbah</em> 50:5) and patristic readings (Ambrose, Augustine) sometimes praise Lot for prioritizing hospitality, sometimes condemn him for sacrificing his daughters. Modern feminist criticism reads the passage as exposing patriarchal society’s expendable view of women. The narrative tension is unresolved — Lot is “righteous” (2 Pet 2:7) yet morally compromised.",
        "User paraphrase; references Ambrose, Augustine",
    ),
