        19,
        13,
        "",
        "the outcry against them",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The outcry of Sodom.</strong> The Hebrew <em>tseʿaqah</em> (<em>צְעָקָה</em>, “outcry”) is the same word used for Israel’s cry from Egypt (Ex 3:7) and the cry of the oppressed throughout the prophets. The grammar is deliberately ambiguous: is the outcry by Sodom (their wickedness reaching God) or against Sodom (the cry of their victims)? Ezekiel 16:49 specifies the latter — Sodom oppressed the poor and needy. The verse models a theology in which the cries of the oppressed reach God and trigger judgment, the inverse of the prophetic insistence that God is bound to hear those cries.",
        "User original",
