        "",
        "Yahweh rained on Sodom",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Fire and brimstone.</strong> The destruction of Sodom by sulfur and fire becomes the paradigm for divine eschatological judgment. Revelation repeatedly invokes “fire and brimstone” (Rev 14:10; 20:10; 21:8) as the fate of the unrepentant. Jesus references Sodom’s fate in his eschatological discourse (Luke 17:29, “the day Lot went out from Sodom, it rained fire and brimstone from the sky”). The geological possibility of an earthquake-released hydrocarbon ignition near the Dead Sea has been proposed by some archaeologists, though most commentators see the narrative’s purpose as theological rather than geological.",
        "User original",
