    (
        20,
        2,
        "",
        "She is my sister",
        "comm-contextual",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The wife-sister theme, again.</strong> The episode is the second of three OT wife-sister narratives: Abraham with Pharaoh (Gen 12:10–20), here with Abimelech of Gerar, and Isaac with Abimelech of Gerar (26:6–11). The repetition has been extensively analyzed: source-critically (different documentary sources preserving variant traditions of one event), narratively (escalating pattern in which the patriarchs increasingly grasp Yahweh’s protection without believing they need it), and theologically (Yahweh’s protection of the matriarchal lineage despite patriarchal cowardice). Speiser’s influential 1955 article connected the practice to Hurrian wife-sister legal arrangements at Nuzi, where high-status wives could acquire formal "sister" status to enhance the wife’s legal standing — though most modern scholarship treats this connection as overdrawn. The narratives portray the patriarchs as morally ambiguous: protected by divine intervention despite their deceptions, with foreign rulers occasionally appearing more honorable than the patriarchs themselves (cf. 20:9, Abimelech’s rebuke).',
        "User original",
    ),
