        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Covering of the eyes.</strong> The Hebrew <em>kesut ʿeinayim</em> (<em>כְּסוּת</em> <em>עֵינַיִם</em>, “covering of the eyes”) is an obscure idiom — the silver is given to Sarah to “cover the eyes” of those who would have judged her. Possibilities: (1) public vindication, the silver as proof of Sarah’s value and Abraham’s genuine ownership; (2) “to deflect attention from” the incident; (3) to “block accusing eyes” with the gift’s substantial value. Rabbinic interpretation (<em>Bereshit Rabbah</em> 52:13) develops the phrase as a kind of forced public acknowledgment of Sarah’s marital status.",
        "User original",
