    (
        21,
        1,
        "",
        "Yahweh visited Sarah as he had said",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Yahweh visited Sarah.</strong> The Hebrew verb <em>paqad</em> (<em>פָּקַד</em>, “visited, attended to”) is theologically loaded — used of God’s visiting his people for blessing (50:24–25, Israel’s Egyptian remembrance) or judgment (Ex 32:34). The same root yields the Septuagint’s <em>episkeptomai</em>, which becomes Luke 1:68’s <em>epeskepsato</em> (“visited his people”) at the Magnificat — explicitly recalling Sarah’s miraculous conception as type of Mary’s. Sarah’s “visitation” inaugurates a biblical pattern: divine action on barren women (Hannah, Elizabeth) marks moments of redemptive history.",
        "User paraphrase; references Septuagint",
    ),
