        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (360).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 360 AD)</small><p>&#x27;My son, God will provide himself a lamb for a burnt offering&#x27;. Ephrem reads this verse as the most direct prophecy of the Crucifixion in the entire Torah: God will provide HIMSELF (i.e., God-incarnate) as the lamb. The substitution-of-ram (22:13) is a temporal stay; the ultimate lamb is the God-man on the Cross. Tewahedo Christology preserves Ephrem&#x27;s reading: the ram caught in the thicket prefigures Christ&#x27;s crown of thorns; Isaac unbound prefigures the empty tomb.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Genesis, Section XXII.8. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13, ed. J. Gwynn / Schaff (1898). PD.",
