        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (360).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 360 AD)</small><p>&#x27;And Sarah died... and Abraham came to mourn for Sarah, and to weep for her&#x27;. Ephrem reads Abraham&#x27;s mourning as the legitimate-grief warrant: faith in the resurrection does NOT abolish the legitimate human grief at death. Tewahedo funeral theology preserves Ephrem&#x27;s balance: mourning is permitted (commanded, even) for forty days; Christian hope reshapes mourning but does not eliminate it. Abraham weeping over Sarah&#x27;s body authorises every Tewahedo funeral weeping.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Genesis, Section XXIII.2. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13, ed. J. Gwynn / Schaff (1898). PD.",
