        11,
        "",
        "In the presence of the children of my people",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The bargaining ritual.</strong> The exchange between Abraham and Ephron follows a formalized ANE bargaining pattern: the seller offers as gift, the buyer insists on paying. The convention serves multiple functions — establishing the buyer’s seriousness, ensuring the seller’s consent is unambiguous, creating witnesses to the transaction. Abraham’s insistence on paying (and Ephron’s “naming” of the price) establishes legal title without ambiguity. The detailed economic procedure underscores that Abraham’s first land-claim is by purchase, not divine grant — a paradox at the heart of patriarchal tenure.",
        "User original",
