        14,
        "",
        "let her be the one you have appointed",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Asking for a sign.</strong> The servant proposes a specific sign: the right woman will offer to water his camels unbidden. The procedure — proposing a fleece-like test to identify divine guidance — recurs in Gideon (Judg 6:36–40). Some commentators see this as model prayer; others as edge-of-superstition fortune-seeking. The narrative validates it: Rebekah arrives mid-prayer (24:15), and her actions exactly match the servant’s proposed sign. The text models a confidence in providential specificity that has been theologically generative and pastorally ambivalent.",
        "User original",
