        25,
        25,
        "",
        "They named him Esau",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Esau and Edom.</strong> Esau (<em>ʿEsav</em>, <em>עֵשָׂו</em>) is named for his hairy (<em>seʿar</em>) appearance — though the Hebrew etymology of the name itself is uncertain. He is also called Edom (red, <em>ʾadom</em>) at 25:30, after the red lentil stew. The doubled name (Esau/Edom) becomes the standard biblical naming of the Edomite people, located south of the Dead Sea, perpetual rivals of Israel (Num 20:14–21; Obadiah). The namings encode the chapter’s theology: identity is established by appearance and appetite, both later overturned by the younger brother.",
        "User original",
