        34,
        "",
        "Esau despised his birthright",
        "comm-patristic",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Despising the birthright.</strong> Hebrews 12:16 takes this verse as paradigm of impiety: “see to it that no one is sexually immoral or godless like Esau, who sold his birthright for a single meal.” The birthright (<em>bekhorah</em>) carried inheritance rights — typically a double share — and headship of the clan. Esau’s exchange of the long-term covenant inheritance for immediate hunger-satisfaction becomes the moral category for valuing the temporal over the eternal. The motif of preferring immediate gratification over eschatological promise is Pauline-influenced and later Augustinian.",
        "User original",
