        "",
        "Abraham obeyed my voice, and kept my requirements",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Abraham as Torah-keeper.</strong> The verse retroactively credits Abraham with keeping all four categories of divine command — voice, requirements (<em>mishmeret</em>), commandments (<em>mitzvot</em>), statutes (<em>chuqqim</em>), laws (<em>torot</em>) — terminology that becomes Torah’s standard vocabulary at Sinai. Rabbinic tradition (m. Kiddushin 4:14) reads this as proof that Abraham kept the entire Torah before it was given. Christian theology has read it differently — Abraham’s “obedience” being faith-trust rather than law-keeping, with Romans 4:13 making faith primary.",
        "User original",
