        "",
        "He built an altar there",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Isaac’s altar.</strong> Isaac is the only patriarch known for building just a single altar (compared to Abraham’s multiple). The altar at Beersheba, paired with calling on Yahweh’s name, follows the Abrahamic pattern (cf. 12:8; 13:18). The minimal altar-building has been read various ways: as Isaac’s quieter character, as the theological transmission being intact rather than expansive, or as the narrative’s lower interest in Isaac compared to his father and son. Beersheba (well of seven / oath) becomes a distinctive Isaac-site (28:10).",
        "User original",
