        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (360).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 360 AD)</small><p>&#x27;See, the smell of my son is as the smell of a field which the Lord hath blessed&#x27;. Ephrem treats Isaac&#x27;s olfactory blessing of Jacob as the prototype of every sacramental aroma: Israel-in-Jacob bears the fragrance of Eden (the field-which-the-Lord-blessed = the Edenic garden, by Ephrem&#x27;s reading). Tewahedo liturgical use of frankincense in the qǝddase preserves this aroma-as-blessing pattern: the church fills with the smell of paradise.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Genesis, Section XXVII.27. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13, ed. J. Gwynn / Schaff (1898). PD.",
