    (
        27,
        33,
        "",
        "Isaac trembled violently",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Isaac’s trembling.</strong> The Hebrew <em>vayecharad…charadah gedolah ʿad-meʾod</em> uses cognate intensification — “he trembled a great trembling, exceedingly” — the strongest emotional vocabulary in the Hebrew Bible. The trembling is not anger but holy dread: Isaac recognizes that providence has overruled his preferred choice. His response — “yes, and he shall be blessed” — affirms Jacob’s blessing rather than revoking it. The patriarchal blessing, once spoken, is irrevocable. The theology of irrevocable speech-act underlies prophetic theology (Isa 55:11; Heb 6:18).",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        27,
        34,
        "",
        "an exceedingly great and bitter cry",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Esau’s bitter cry.</strong> Esau’s <em>tseʿaqah gedolah u-marah ʿad-meʾod</em> is a strikingly intense lament — the same vocabulary used elsewhere of national mourning (Esther 4:1) and of Israel’s cry under bondage (Ex 3:7). Hebrews 12:17 cites this verse as paradigm of irreversible loss: “he was rejected, for he found no place to repent, though he sought it with tears.” The juxtaposition is theologically severe — Esau weeps but cannot reverse. The verse has shaped Christian theology of unforgivable opportunity, though the original Hebrew context is regret over lost blessing rather than damnation.",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        27,
        36,
        "",
        "Isn’t he rightly named Jacob?",
        "word",
        "Hebrew",
        "Hebrew",
        '<strong>Ya‘aqov (<em>יַעֲקֹב</em>) — "supplanter."</strong> Esau’s bitter pun on his brother’s name plays on the Hebrew root <em>‘qb</em>, which means "heel" but in verb form means "to seize by the heel, to supplant, to deceive." Jacob (<em>Ya‘aqov</em>) was so named because he came out grasping Esau’s heel at birth (25:26); now Esau retroactively reads the name as predictive: "twice he has supplanted (<em>ya‘qveni</em>) me" — taking the birthright in ch 25, the blessing in ch 27. The naming-becomes-destiny pattern is one of Genesis’s recurring literary devices: names predict (Jacob, Esau, Edom, Babel, etc.). Hosea later picks up the same wordplay: "in the womb he supplanted (<em>‘aqav</em>) his brother" (Hos 12:3). The pun is also theological: Jacob’s name will eventually be changed to "Israel" (32:28), explicitly contrasted with the supplanting-name. The change marks the central theological transformation of the Jacob narrative — from supplanter to wrestler-with-God.',
        "User original",
    ),
