    (
        28,
        17,
        "c",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (360).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 360 AD)</small><p>&#x27;How dreadful is this place! this is none other but the house of God, and this is the gate of heaven&#x27;. Ephrem reads Bethel (&#x27;house of God&#x27;) as the proto-temple, proto-church, proto-tabot. Tewahedo ecclesiology explicitly carries the gate-of-heaven identification through every Tewahedo church: each parish church is in some sense Bethel — a renewed-Jacob place where heaven opens. The Tewahedo qǝddase opens with this consciousness.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Genesis, Section XXVIII.17. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13, ed. J. Gwynn / Schaff (1898). PD.",
    ),
    (
        28,
        17,
        "m",
        "",
        "word",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Mah nora ha-maqom ha-zeh (<em>מַה־נּוֹרָא</em> <em>הַמָּקוֹם</em> <em>הַזֶּה</em>).</strong> The root <em>y-r-a</em> denotes the trembling reverence proper to encountering the holy. The verse coined the Jewish liturgical phrase used at synagogue dedications and holiness moments. <em>Maqom</em>, "place," later becomes a rabbinic divine name — "The Place" = God who is the place of all places.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        28,
        18,
        "",
        "set it up for a pillar",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The standing stone.</strong> Jacob’s stone-pillar (<em>matzevah</em>) marks the site as sacred. Standing stones (<em>matzevot</em>) were a widespread ANE religious feature, attested archaeologically at many cult sites in Canaan. The Mosaic law later prohibits them (Deut 16:22; Lev 26:1) precisely because of their Canaanite cultic associations — but Jacob’s pillar predates the prohibition. The oil-anointing transforms a stone into a covenant-marker, the gesture of consecration that gives the Hebrew word <em>mashiach</em> (anointed) its meaning. The site is renamed <em>Beit El</em> — “house of God.”",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        28,
        18,
        "a",
        "",
        "topic-nave",
        "Topic",
        "Topic.",
        "<strong>Topics.</strong> This verse appears under: IN CONSECRATION, PILLAR, RISING, STONES. <em>[Reviewer: pick one topic (typically the first or most theologically loaded), write a 2–3 sentence thematic note, and discard the rest.]</em>",
        "Nave's Topical Bible, Orville J. Nave (1896). Public domain.",
    ),
    (
        28,
        20,
        "",
        "Jacob vowed a vow",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The first explicit human vow.</strong> Jacob’s vow (<em>neder</em>) is the first explicitly named human vow in Scripture. The Hebrew <em>neder</em> creates a binding self-obligation; vows become a major category in biblical law (Num 30; Deut 23:21–23) and rabbinic ethics. The conditional structure (“If God will be with me… then Yahweh will be my God”) has occasioned debate: is Jacob bargaining? Most commentators read the conditional as not bargaining but recognition — Jacob accepts the divine promise just made (28:13–15) and vows responsive worship. The pattern of vow-as-response-to-revelation recurs throughout the Hebrew Bible.",
        "User original",
    ),
