        "you are my bone and my flesh",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Bone and flesh.</strong> Laban’s recognition of Jacob as “bone and flesh” uses the same idiom Adam used of Eve (2:23) and David’s tribes use of the king (2 Sam 5:1). The phrase identifies kinship — not metaphor but legal-genealogical claim. Laban’s invocation seems hospitable but the next chapters reveal manipulation: he accepts Jacob’s labor for fourteen years on the basis of this kinship without paying wages until forced. The phrase’s reuse here, in light of its Edenic origin, ironizes covenant kinship — bone-and-flesh language can mask exploitation as much as honor solidarity.",
        "User original",
