        20,
        "",
        "They seemed to him but a few days",
        "comm-patristic",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Time transformed by love.</strong> The verse’s compression — seven years pass like a few days — is among the Bible’s most famous expressions of how love transforms temporal experience. The narrative voice rarely intrudes for romantic comment; here it does. The phenomenology of love-distorted time has shaped Christian mystical and philosophical reflection (Augustine, <em>Confessions</em> XI; the Song of Songs). The verse will be ironized by what follows — the night Jacob discovers Leah, time will reverse and the seven-year preparation will produce wrong outcome through Laban’s deception.",
        "User paraphrase; references Augustine",
