    (
        30,
        14,
        "",
        "mandrakes",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The mandrake episode.</strong> Mandrakes (Hebrew <em>duda’im</em>) — yellowish fruits of <em>Mandragora officinarum</em>, the root vaguely man-shaped — were widely understood across the ANE as fertility-aphrodisiacs and aids to conception. The episode is one of the OT’s most strikingly domestic-biological scenes: Reuben finds mandrakes in the field, Rachel (the barren-favored wife) trades a night with Jacob to Leah (the fertile-rejected wife) for them. The transaction reveals the depth of the wife-rivalry that drives the chapter: Rachel will sell her marital privilege for what she hopes will fix her infertility. The episode does not work as Rachel hopes — Leah conceives Issachar from this very night (30:17–18), while Rachel’s eventual conception of Joseph (30:22) is attributed to Yahweh "remembering" her, not to mandrakes. The narrative quietly subverts the magical assumption: divine action, not folk medicine, opens the womb. Song 7:13 mentions mandrakes again in an erotic context, confirming the cultural association.',
        "User original",
    ),
