        "stolen my gods",
        "comm-contextual",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Laban’s gods.</strong> Laban’s reaction reveals the religious texture of his household: he genuinely believes the teraphim are “my gods” — not merely cultural artifacts. The patriarchal narratives quietly attest to polytheism within Abraham’s own extended family (cf. Joshua 24:2, “your fathers… served other gods”). Jacob’s family is in contact with this older household religion through Rachel and Leah. The Laban incident frames the long process of Israel’s emergence from polytheistic Mesopotamian context — a process Joshua’s farewell speech (Josh 24:14–15) explicitly recalls.",
        "User original",
