        "the fear of Isaac",
        "comm-contextual",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The Fear of Isaac.</strong> The unusual divine epithet <em>Pachad Yitzhak</em> (<em>פַּחַד</em> <em>יִצְחָק</em>, “Fear of Isaac”) appears here and at 31:53. Three readings dominate: (1) “fear” = the One feared/worshiped by Isaac, an objective genitive; (2) “fear” = numinous awe, the Otto <em>tremendum</em>; (3) connected to Akkadian <em>puchru</em> (assembly), the God of Isaac’s clan. Albright’s scholarship developed option 3, identifying the epithet with patriarchal clan-deity religion. The phrase is one of several distinctive patriarchal divine names (Mighty One of Jacob, Shield of Abraham) preserved in oldest poetic strata.",
        "User original",
