        "",
        "fear of his father, Isaac",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Distinct ancestral deities.</strong> Laban swears by “the God of Abraham, and the God of Nahor, the God of their father” — Jacob swears by “the Fear of his father Isaac.” The asymmetric divine identifications are theologically significant: each side invokes its own ancestral god. The “God of their father” may simply be the unnamed god common to both Abraham and Nahor, or distinct deities. The text preserves a memory of clan-religion before strict monotheism — each patriarchal line associated with its own God-relationship, eventually unified under the name Yahweh.",
        "User original",
