        "This is God’s army",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Mahanaim — two camps.</strong> Jacob meets a band of angels and names the place <em>Mahanaim</em> (“two camps”). The doubled name suggests the meeting of heavenly and earthly hosts. The site reappears in 2 Samuel 17:24 as David’s refuge during Absalom’s rebellion. The Mahanaim encounter functions narratively as inclusio with Bethel (28:12, the angel-stairway) — angels frame Jacob’s exile, marking both the journey out and the journey home. The narrative treats angelic encounter as a regular feature of patriarchal experience rather than rare miracle.",
        "User original",
