    (
        32,
        10,
        "",
        "I am not worthy of the least of all the loving kindnesses",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Jacob’s prayer.</strong> Jacob’s prayer the night before meeting Esau is one of Genesis’s most emotionally exposed moments. The Hebrew opening — <em>qatonti mi-kol ha-ḥasadim</em>, "I am too small for all the loving-kindnesses" — uses an unusual self-diminishing formula that registers genuine humility: Jacob is acknowledging that the prosperity Yahweh has given him exceeds what he deserves. The prayer’s theological structure is exemplary: invocation of Yahweh as covenant-God of his fathers (32:9), self-abasement (32:10), specific request for protection (32:11), and appeal to divine promise (32:12). The chapter overall is one of the OT’s most carefully constructed sequences: Jacob’s elaborate gifts to Esau (32:13–21), the dispatch of his family across the Jabbok (32:22–23), and finally the all-night wrestling-match with the divine figure (32:24–32). The prayer functions as the chapter’s pivot — Jacob has to come to grips with his own inadequacy before he can face either Esau or the night-stranger. The verse encodes one of the OT’s clearest portraits of authentic prayer.',
        "User original",
    ),
