    (
        33,
        4,
        "",
        "Esau ran to meet him, embraced him",
        "comm-modern-critical",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Esau’s embrace.</strong> The reunion is one of Genesis’s most emotionally complex scenes: Jacob has spent the previous chapter terrified of Esau’s vengeance, dividing his camp for safety, sending elaborate gifts, wrestling with God all night for the strength to face his brother — and Esau simply runs, embraces, kisses, and weeps. The expected violence does not materialize. The brother who was supposed to kill Jacob (cf. 27:41) instead enacts forgiveness so complete that Jacob says "I have seen your face as one sees the face of God, and you have accepted me" (33:10). The line connects the encounter with Esau directly to Jacob’s previous-night encounter with God at Peniel (32:30, "I have seen God face to face"). The parallel is theological: Jacob meets God in his struggling brother just as he met God at the ford. Esau, in his moment of unexpected mercy, becomes the divine face. The episode is one of the OT’s most extraordinary portraits of forgiveness — not earned, not negotiated, simply offered. Walter Brueggemann reads ch 33 as one of the canon’s most surprising theological moments.',
        "User paraphrase; summarises Brueggemann",
    ),
