        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (360).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 360 AD)</small><p>&#x27;And Esau ran to meet him, and embraced him, and fell on his neck, and kissed him: and they wept&#x27;. Ephrem reads the reconciliation of the brothers as a foretaste of the eschatological reconciliation promised in Rom 11:25-26 (the fullness of the Gentiles, then all Israel saved). Tewahedo ecumenical theology — preserved in the long Tewahedo refusal to demonize other communions even during Chalcedonian controversies — draws on the Esau-Jacob embrace as canonical model for inter-Christian relations.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Genesis, Section XXXIII.4. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13, ed. J. Gwynn / Schaff (1898). PD.",
