        "the pace of the livestock",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Pace of the cattle.</strong> Jacob’s gentle excuse — proceeding at the pace of cattle and children — is sometimes read as continuing deception (Jacob never actually goes to Seir to meet Esau; he settles in Succoth, 33:17). Other readings see Jacob as genuinely thoughtful caretaker, refusing to push his vulnerable household at warrior-pace. The phrase has become proverbial for slow caretaking leadership — adopted by Pope Gregory the Great in his <em>Pastoral Rule</em> on the bishop as shepherd (Bk II.5). Whether sincere or evasive, the verse models patient leadership.",
        "User original",
