        "in peace to the city of Shechem",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Jacob arrives in peace.</strong> The Hebrew <em>shalem</em> here is grammatically ambiguous — it can mean “in peace” (as a state) or “Salem” (a place name, possibly Salim near Shechem). The “in peace” reading frames the verse as the climax of Jacob’s exile journey: he left in fear (28:10) and returns in peace. The verse’s sequel (the Dinah catastrophe of ch 34) immediately complicates the “peace” — Jacob’s peace is fragile and short-lived. The narrative structure (peace immediately followed by violation) becomes paradigmatic in the Hebrew Bible: settled peace is never the end of the story.",
        "User original",
