    (
        33,
        20,
        "",
        "El Elohe Israel",
        "word",
        "Hebrew",
        "Hebrew",
        '<strong>El Elohe Yisrael (<em>אֵל</em> <em>אֱלֹהֵי</em> <em>יִשְׂרָאֵל</em>) — "God, the God of Israel."</strong> Jacob’s altar at Shechem bears one of Genesis’s most theologically dense names: El (the broader Northwest Semitic divine name, used as title or proper name across Canaanite religion) is named as <em>Elohei Yisra’el</em> ("the God of Israel"). The naming registers Jacob’s identity-shift after Peniel — he is now Israel, and his altar identifies the deity as <em>his</em> God, the God specifically of his line. The construction "El the God of Israel" also works theologically as identification: the high god El of the broader Levantine pantheon is named here as functionally identical with the deity who has shepherded Jacob. Most modern OT scholarship (so Cross, Smith) reads such constructions as evidence of the gradual Israelite absorption of El-traditions into Yahwism — Yahweh and El were not always identified, but the patriarchal narratives increasingly identify them as the same. Jacob’s altar-name preserves an older theological moment, before that identification was fully developed. The verse is one of the OT’s most important pieces of evidence for the historical development of Israelite monotheism.',
        "User original",
    ),
