        "he had done folly in Israel",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Folly in Israel.</strong> The phrase <em>nevalah be-Yisraʾel</em> (<em>נְבָלָה</em> <em>בְיִשְׂרָאֵל</em>, “folly in Israel”) is a technical Hebrew term for a serious moral and social outrage — used of the Levite’s concubine (Judg 19:23–24), Achan’s transgression (Josh 7:15), Tamar’s rape (2 Sam 13:12). The word <em>nevalah</em> denotes far more than “folly” — it is a category-violation, an act that disrupts communal order. The phrase here serves as the Bible’s first appearance of this technical term — and remarkably, it predates the formation of Israel as a people, indicating the editorial perspective is later than the events narrated.",
