        "when they were sore",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The third-day attack.</strong> The post-circumcision third day was when wound infection peaked — the Shechemite men were physically incapacitated, unable to defend themselves. The detail underscores the calculated nature of Simeon and Levi’s attack. The “third day” motif resonates with later biblical usage (Exodus 19:11, the third-day theophany at Sinai; Hosea 6:2, “after two days he will revive us; on the third day he will raise us up”) — but inverted here as a day of violence rather than restoration. The pattern of using sacred timing for unholy purpose anticipates prophetic critique of weaponized religion.",
