        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (360).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 360 AD)</small><p>&#x27;And God said unto him, Thy name is Jacob: thy name shall not be called any more Jacob, but Israel shall be thy name&#x27;. Ephrem notes the divine reconfirmation: the angelic Jabbok name-change is now RATIFIED by God Himself in direct theophany. The pattern of double-confirmation (angelic-then-divine) is preserved in Tewahedo sacramental practice: the deacon&#x27;s prayer precedes the priest&#x27;s consecration; the bishop&#x27;s chrismation ratifies the presbyter&#x27;s baptism. Authority moves in stages, never bypassed.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Genesis, Section XXXV.10. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13, ed. J. Gwynn / Schaff (1898). PD.",
