    (
        35,
        18,
        "c",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (360).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 360 AD)</small><p>&#x27;And it came to pass, as her soul was in departing, (for she died,) that she called his name Ben-oni: but his father called him Benjamin&#x27;. Ephrem reads the double-naming as the dialectic of grief-and-hope: the mother dying names her son Son-of-my-sorrow; the father renames him Son-of-the-right-hand (i.e., Son-of-strength / favoured-son). Tewahedo pastoral theology preserves this pattern: the church speaks both griefs honestly AND reframes them into hope, never denying the first.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Genesis, Section XXXV.18. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13, ed. J. Gwynn / Schaff (1898). PD.",
    ),
    (
        35,
        19,
        "",
        "Rachel died, and was buried",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Rachel’s tomb.</strong> Rachel is the only matriarch buried outside Machpelah — buried instead on the road to Bethlehem (35:19). Jeremiah 31:15 invokes her: “a voice is heard in Ramah, lamentation and bitter weeping, Rachel weeping for her children.” Matthew 2:18 cites this verse at the slaughter of the innocents — Rachel’s geographic burial-place becomes the eschatological figure of all maternal grief. The tomb (now in modern Bethlehem) remains a major Jewish pilgrimage site. The geography is theological: Rachel buried at the gate of Bethlehem positions her as eternal weeper for the line that will produce David and Christ.",
        "User original",
    ),
