    (
        35,
        29,
        "",
        "Isaac gave up the spirit and died",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Isaac’s death.</strong> Isaac dies at 180, the longest-lived of the patriarchs (Abraham 175, Jacob 147). The verse pairs his death with burial by both sons — Esau and Jacob together, in apparent reconciliation. The detail echoes 25:9 (Isaac and Ishmael burying Abraham together). Family conflicts that dominate the patriarchal narratives are framed by funerary cooperation — death produces a brief solidarity that life could not sustain. Isaac’s narrative ends quietly; the chapter following turns immediately to the line of Esau (ch 36) before resuming the line of Jacob (ch 37).",
        "User original",
    ),
