        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (360).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 360 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Now these are the generations of Esau, who is Edom&#x27;. Ephrem reads the Edom-genealogy as the FAITHFUL recording of the rejected line — Scripture preserves Esau&#x27;s descendants not to praise but to remember. Tewahedo historiographical tradition preserves the principle: even the rejected names are recorded, because divine memory excludes no one. The Mäṣḥafä Sǝnksar (Tewahedo Synaxarium) preserves names of heretics and apostates in this same spirit — for memory, not for veneration.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Genesis, Section XXXVI.1. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13, ed. J. Gwynn / Schaff (1898). PD.",
