    (
        36,
        2,
        "",
        "Now this is the history of the generations of Esau",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Esau’s toledot.</strong> Genesis 36 is one of two genealogical chapters devoted to Esau (the other is the king-list at 36:31–43), reflecting the editorial principle that disinherited or non-chosen lines receive their genealogy <em>before</em> the chosen line continues. The pattern recurs: Cain’s line (4:17–22) before Seth’s; Ishmael (25:12–18) before Isaac; Esau (here) before Jacob (37:2). The structural choice has theological weight — even non-chosen lines are recorded with care, suggesting that election is selective rather than annihilating, and that the rejected branch’s history matters.",
        "User original",
    ),
