        "",
        "into a land away from his brother Jacob",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Geographic separation.</strong> Esau’s withdrawal from the land — too crowded for both — formally cedes Canaan to Jacob’s line. The phrasing echoes Abraham and Lot’s earlier separation (13:9–11) where Lot chose the better-watered Jordan plain. Both scenes establish boundaries by departure rather than treaty. Esau’s settling in Seir (the future Edom, southeast of the Dead Sea) creates the geographic stage for the long Israelite-Edomite enmity that culminates in the prophetic oracles against Edom (Obadiah; Mal 1:2–4).",
        "User original",
