    (
        36,
        8,
        "",
        "Esau is Edom",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Esau as Edom.</strong> The chapter is the OT’s most extended Edomite genealogy, identifying Esau as the eponymous ancestor of Edom — the people who occupied the highlands south and east of the Dead Sea (Mount Seir). The fraternal-rival relationship between Israel and Edom runs through the entire OT: Edomite resistance during the wilderness period (Num 20:14–21), David’s subjugation of Edom (2 Sam 8:13–14), Edom’s opportunistic violence against Judah during the Babylonian destruction (Ps 137:7, Obadiah’s entire prophecy), and the Maccabean-era forced conversion of Idumea (the Greek name for Edom). The chapter’s placement here — extended Edomite genealogy before the resumption of Joseph-cycle narrative in ch 37 — frames the ongoing canonical contrast: Esau’s line becomes a kingdom rapidly (36:31, "before any king reigned over the children of Israel"); Jacob’s line becomes Israel slowly. The chapter’s detail registers the OT’s recurring fascination with the elder-brother lineage: even when not chosen, Esau’s descendants matter, and Genesis preserves their record in detail.',
        "User original",
    ),
