    (
        36,
        31,
        "",
        "before any king reigned over the children of Israel",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The Edomite kings list.</strong> The chapter lists eight kings of Edom by name with succession pattern, then tribal chieftains. The introductory formula — "before any king reigned over the children of Israel" — is one of the OT’s strongest internal indicators of post-monarchic dating: the editor is writing from a vantage point at which Israel <em>does</em> have kings, looking back to a period when Edom did but Israel did not. Most modern critical scholarship treats the formula as one of the clearest signals that Genesis as a finished work cannot be earlier than Saul’s reign (the first Israelite king, ca. 1020 BCE), with most placing the final compilation considerably later. The chapter is therefore unusually transparent about its own historical perspective. Theologically, the verse registers a striking observation: Esau’s line organized into stable monarchy long before Jacob’s line did. The OT does not interpret this as Esau’s superiority; rather, monarchy itself is theologically ambivalent in the OT (1 Sam 8). Edom’s precocity in monarchy is, on the deuteronomistic reading, not an advantage but an early symptom of a different political-religious trajectory.',
        "User original",
    ),
