    (
        37,
        2,
        "",
        "an evil report",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Joseph’s evil report.</strong> The Hebrew <em>dibbah raʿah</em> (<em>דִּבָּה</em> <em>רָעָה</em>, “evil report, slander”) is morally ambiguous in this verse. Was Joseph reporting his brothers’ actual misconduct (which would be morally licit) or maliciously slandering them? The text leaves it ambiguous, but the chapter’s psychology suggests the brothers experienced his reports as betrayal. The same vocabulary (<em>dibbah</em>) is used of the spies’ false report (Num 14:36) and prophetic critique. The verse opens the Joseph cycle by establishing fraternal tension as a moral-psychological reality, not merely behavioral conflict.",
        "User original",
    ),
