    (
        37,
        3,
        "a",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (360).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 360 AD)</small><p>&#x27;Now Israel loved Joseph more than all his children... and he made him a coat of many colours&#x27;. Ephrem reads the ketonet-passim (coat of many colours / long-sleeved tunic) as a PRIESTLY garment — the Septuagint&#x27;s poikilon resonates with priestly vestment language. Jacob&#x27;s gift to Joseph is implicit priestly-investiture. Tewahedo liturgical vestments preserve the multi-coloured-tunic pattern in the deacon&#x27;s and priest&#x27;s robes: the colours are not decorative but PROPHETIC, drawing on Joseph&#x27;s typology.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Genesis, Section XXXVII.3. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13, ed. J. Gwynn / Schaff (1898). PD.",
    ),
    (
        37,
        3,
        "m",
        "",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Joseph’s coat.</strong> The Hebrew <em>k’tonet passim</em> appears only here and in 2 Sam 13:18, where it describes a princess’s garment. "Many colors" (KJV) is from the LXX <em>chitōna poikilon</em>; the Hebrew probably means "long-sleeved" or "ornamented" — a robe of distinction, marking Joseph as Jacob’s appointed heir over his brothers. Its tearing and bloodying (37:31–33) becomes the symbol of fraternal betrayal.',
        "User paraphrase; references LXX",
    ),
