    (
        37,
        7,
        "",
        "binding sheaves in the field",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The sheaves dream.</strong> The first of Joseph’s two prophetic dreams uses agricultural imagery — sheaves bowing — that fits a settled-agriculture milieu, not yet Joseph’s eventual Egyptian context. The dream’s prediction is fulfilled at Genesis 42:6 (the brothers bow before the Egyptian governor). The dream-as-prophecy structure becomes paradigmatic for biblical prophetic dreams: not ambiguous but disclosive of future divine plan. Joseph’s two dreams (sheaves, then sun-moon-stars) escalate in cosmic scope — the same pattern of dream-pair structures Pharaoh’s dreams (cows, then grain) at Genesis 41.",
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        37,
        9,
        "",
        "the sun and the moon and eleven stars",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Sun, moon, and stars.</strong> Joseph’s second dream uses cosmic imagery — celestial bodies bowing. Father Jacob immediately interprets sun and moon as himself and Rachel, eleven stars as the eleven brothers. The motif’s literary afterlife is rich: Revelation 12:1 reuses the imagery (“a woman clothed with the sun, with the moon under her feet, and on her head a crown of twelve stars”) — typically read as referring to Israel-as-Mary. The eleven-stars-plus-Joseph make twelve, the tribal number. The dream encodes Joseph’s eventual ruling status while paradoxically fueling the brothers’ hatred that brings about its fulfillment.",
        "User original",
    ),
