    (
        37,
        24,
        "",
        "The pit was empty. There was no water in it",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The empty pit.</strong> The detail “no water in it” signals a dry cistern — depth without drowning, suitable for imprisonment but not for execution. The pit (<em>bor</em>) functions as proto-grave imagery throughout the Hebrew Bible — Joseph’s pit foreshadows Jeremiah’s cistern (Jer 38:6), the “pit” of Sheol (Ps 30:3), and ultimately the tomb. Christian typology has read Joseph’s descent into and emergence from the pit as type of Christ’s death and resurrection. The chapter’s literary structure — descent into pit, sale into Egypt, eventual rise to power — establishes Joseph as paradigmatic suffering-then-glorified figure.",
        "User original",
    ),
