    (
        37,
        28,
        "",
        "sold Joseph to the Ishmaelites for twenty pieces of silver",
        "comm-contextual",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The sale price.</strong> Twenty shekels of silver was the standard price of a young male slave in ANE slave-markets of the Middle Bronze Age — attested in the Code of Hammurabi (§116) and in Mesopotamian and Egyptian commercial records. The narrative detail is precisely period-appropriate; later periods saw slave-prices rise (Lev 27:5 sets the dedication-redemption value of a male 5–20 years old at 20 shekels; Exod 21:32 sets the gore-by-ox compensation for a slave at 30 shekels in a slightly later period). The verse is one of the OT’s small windows on the actual slave-market economics of the patriarchal era, and the price’s historical accuracy is one of several details supporting an early second-millennium-BCE date for the underlying tradition. The Christological echo — Judas’ betrayal price of thirty pieces of silver (Matt 26:15, also citing Zech 11:12–13) — has been read as deliberate intertextual signaling: Judas selling Jesus parallels the brothers selling Joseph, who was their predecessor as righteous-sufferer-vindicated. The verse is the moment the entire Joseph-cycle plot turns on.",
        "User paraphrase; references Code of Hammurabi",
    ),
