    (
        38,
        1,
        "",
        "Judah went down from his brothers",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Judah’s narrative interruption.</strong> Genesis 38 is famously inserted between Joseph’s sale (ch 37) and his arrival in Egypt (ch 39), interrupting the Joseph narrative just at its dramatic high point. Various interpreters propose different rationales: showing what was happening on the home front during Joseph’s exile; contrasting Judah’s moral failure with Joseph’s faithfulness in Egypt; establishing Judah’s tribal genealogy (which is the messianic line, not Joseph’s). The chapter’s apparent narrative “digression” is theologically central — Perez, the son born to Tamar and Judah, is direct ancestor of David and ultimately of Jesus (Matt 1:3).",
        "User original",
    ),
