        38,
        14,
        "",
        "covered herself with her veil",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Tamar’s veil.</strong> Tamar’s veiling parallels Rebekah’s (24:65), but here functions as disguise rather than modesty — Tamar uses the veil to be mistaken for a prostitute. The tension between modesty and seduction in the same garment is theologically loaded: covering can be revealing depending on context. The veil’s role in the deception eventually serves justice — Tamar gets the offspring legally owed her by Judah’s family. The narrative’s moral evaluation is supplied at 38:26 (“She is more righteous than I”), making this one of the Bible’s clearest cases of female-initiative justice within patriarchal society.",
        "User original",
