    (
        38,
        26,
        "",
        "She is more righteous than I",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Judah’s admission.</strong> Tamar’s pregnancy by her father-in-law Judah — through her successful disguise as a prostitute when Judah had failed to give her his third son in levirate marriage — is one of the OT’s most morally complex sub-narratives. Judah’s confession ("she is more righteous than I") acknowledges what the chapter has been building: Tamar’s desperate scheme is theologically vindicated as the proper claim on levirate-obligation that Judah was failing to honor. The episode is structurally important: the chapter is inserted into the Joseph cycle (interrupting the seamless narrative between 37:36 and 39:1) for theological reasons. From this Tamar-Judah union come Perez and Zerah (38:29–30), and from Perez descends the Davidic line (Ruth 4:18–22, 1 Chr 2:5–15). Tamar becomes one of the four named women in the genealogy of Christ (Matt 1:3) — alongside Rahab, Ruth, and "the wife of Uriah." The chapter is the OT’s clearest demonstration that the messianic line runs through morally complex circumstances rather than around them. The verse is the canonical vindication of Tamar’s righteousness.',
        "User original",
    ),
