    (
        39,
        2,
        "",
        "Yahweh was with Joseph",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Yahweh with Joseph.</strong> The phrase <em>Yahweh ʿim Yosef</em> (“Yahweh was with Joseph”) appears four times in this chapter (39:2, 3, 21, 23), forming the chapter’s theological refrain. The same formula structures the Davidic narratives (1 Sam 18:14, “David was prosperous in all his ways, and Yahweh was with him”) and reaches Christological climax at Matthew 1:23 (“Emmanuel, which means God with us”). Joseph’s Egyptian exile becomes the founding paradigm of divine presence in foreign land — God’s presence is not territory-bound but person-bound. The motif sustains exilic theology and the New Testament’s universalist mission.",
        "User original",
    ),
