        "set her eyes on Joseph",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The seductive gaze.</strong> Potiphar’s wife’s “lifting of her eyes” to Joseph (Hebrew <em>vatissaʾ … ʿeyneha</em>) is the standard biblical idiom for active sexual interest. Tellingly, the text presents her as the active seducer — a reversal of typical ANE narrative where men pursue. The story’s pattern (faithful Joseph resists, woman accuses falsely, Joseph imprisoned) parallels the ancient Egyptian “Tale of Two Brothers” — making this one of the clearest cases of Hebrew Bible adaptation of Egyptian narrative material. The Hebrew narrative theologizes the type by introducing Yahweh as ground of Joseph’s resistance.",
        "User original",
