        21,
        "",
        "Yahweh was with Joseph",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>"Yahweh was with him."</strong> The phrase ("the LORD was with Joseph") forms one of the chapter’s structural refrains, occurring at 39:2, 21, and 23 — bracketing both the rise in Potiphar’s house and the descent into prison. The narrative point is theologically subtle: the same divine presence that made Joseph prosper in Potiphar’s house follows him into the prison and produces favor with the warden there too. Yahweh’s being-with does not exempt Joseph from suffering or false accusation; it operates within those circumstances rather than removing them. The chapter is one of the OT’s sharpest articulations of providence-through-suffering — a theological pattern that becomes structural in OT and NT both (Job, the Servant Songs of Isa 40–55, Christ’s passion). The Joseph cycle as a whole works out this principle at length: every apparent setback (sold into slavery, falsely imprisoned, forgotten by the cupbearer) becomes the necessary precondition for the next stage of his eventual rise to Pharaoh’s second-in-command. The verse is the chapter’s theological center.',
