        "",
        "each man his dream, in one night",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Paired dreams.</strong> The cup-bearer and baker share the same night for distinct dreams — a literary doubling that creates dramatic tension. Each dream awaits interpretation; their interpretations diverge sharply (one to restoration, one to execution). The pattern recurs at chapter 41 with Pharaoh’s paired dreams (cows + grain) — both meaning the same thing, doubled for emphasis (41:32). The Joseph cycle uses dream-pairs as a structural feature: Joseph’s two dreams (37), prisoners’ two dreams (40), Pharaoh’s two dreams (41). Each dream-pair expands in cosmic significance from family to royal household to nation.",
        "User original",
