    (
        40,
        8,
        "",
        "interpretations belong to God",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The dream-interpretation principle.</strong> Joseph’s answer to the cupbearer and baker — "do not interpretations belong to God?" — establishes the entire theological framework for the dream sequences in chs 40–41. The principle is precise: dream-content comes from God; correct interpretation also comes from God; the human interpreter is merely the divinely enabled mediator. The principle distinguishes Joseph’s practice from professional Egyptian dream-interpreters (Egyptian dream books were extensive, with elaborate symbolic dictionaries; cf. the Chester Beatty dream-papyri). Joseph rejects the technical-professional model in favor of a charismatic-revelational one: each interpretation requires fresh divine illumination. The same principle structures the Daniel narratives (Dan 2:27–28, "no wise men… can show the king the mystery… but there is a God in heaven who reveals secrets") and informs the broader OT theology of prophecy as distinct from divination. The verse is one of the OT’s clearest single-line articulations of the prophet-as-vehicle theology that runs through the entire prophetic tradition.',
        "User original",
    ),
