        41,
        14,
        "",
        "shaved himself, changed his clothing",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Shaving and changing.</strong> Joseph’s preparation for Pharaoh — shaving, changing clothes — reflects Egyptian custom rather than Hebrew (Hebrews maintained beards as standard). Egyptian aristocracy and priesthood were clean-shaven; Joseph’s cultural adaptation marks his transition into Egyptian court status. The shave also indicates a fresh role: he leaves behind the prison identity and begins the courtier identity. The triple clothing-motif (Joseph’s bloody tunic, garment left with Potiphar’s wife, here new garments for Pharaoh) marks his trajectory from family-marked to slave-stripped to court-clothed — eventually culminating in Pharaoh’s signet and fine linen at 41:42.",
        "User original",
