        "Zaphenath-Paneah",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Joseph’s Egyptian name.</strong> Pharaoh renames Joseph <em>Tsafenat-Paneach</em> — variously interpreted as “revealer of secrets,” “the god speaks, he lives,” or other Egyptian etymologies. The renaming completes Joseph’s transformation from Hebrew slave to Egyptian official; he also receives an Egyptian wife (Asenath, daughter of Potiphera, priest of On). The dual identity (Joseph + Tsafenat) parallels the Daniel/Belteshazzar pattern in Babylon. The Joseph cycle thus models faithful service in foreign court while retaining covenant identity — a model that becomes especially relevant for Diaspora Jewish self-understanding under Persian, Greek, and Roman rule.",
        "User original",
