        "bowed themselves down to him",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The brothers bow.</strong> The brothers’ prostration before Joseph fulfills his sheaves-dream of 37:7 — the brothers bowing down to him. Joseph immediately recognizes the fulfillment (42:9, “Joseph remembered the dreams”). The inclusio between dreams and fulfillment is one of the Hebrew Bible’s most extended dream-prophecy structures, separated by 20+ years of narrative. The pattern teaches biblical providence: dreams that seem absurd at the time of receipt are confirmed by their improbable fulfillment. The brothers do not yet know they fulfill the dream — making the moment heavily ironic in narrative terms.",
        "User original",
