    (
        42,
        21,
        "",
        "We are certainly guilty concerning our brother",
        "comm-patristic",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The brothers’ awakening.</strong> Twenty years after selling Joseph into Egyptian slavery, the brothers — confronted by the apparently arbitrary cruelty of "the Egyptian governor" (Joseph in disguise) — recognize their predicament as moral consequence. The Hebrew is striking: "we are guilty (<em>asham</em>) concerning our brother" — the technical legal-ritual word for incurred guilt requiring expiation. The episode is one of the OT’s clearest portraits of long-delayed conscience: the moral reality of an act buried for two decades suddenly emerges in the apparent injustice of a present situation. Joseph, hearing this through an interpreter, has to leave the room to weep (42:24) — the first of his weepings before the brothers in the cycle. The chapter is one of the OT’s sharpest psychological-theological observations: guilt that has been suppressed eventually surfaces, and the surfacing is sometimes triggered by suffering that resembles the suffering originally inflicted. Augustine’s reading of conscience in the Confessions draws explicitly on Joseph-cycle patterns of buried-then-emerging memory.',
        "User paraphrase; references Augustine",
    ),
