        "",
        "He turned himself away from them, and wept",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Joseph’s tears.</strong> Joseph’s secret weeping is one of three tears-scenes in the reunion narrative (here; 43:30; 45:2). Each escalates: hidden weeping, retreat to weep, public weeping. The text humanizes the Egyptian governor — stripping away the official persona to reveal the wounded brother. Patristic and rabbinic readings have noted Joseph’s emotional control: he weeps in private but maintains the test in public. The interplay of love and necessary trial structures the entire reunion sequence — Joseph cannot yet reveal himself because the brothers have not yet completed their moral transformation.",
        "User original",
