    (
        42,
        38,
        "",
        "My son shall not go down with you",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Jacob’s refusal.</strong> Jacob’s flat refusal to send Benjamin to Egypt with the brothers — even when the family is starving — registers the depth of his trauma over Joseph: "his brother is dead, and he only is left." The narrator preserves Jacob’s misperception throughout — Jacob assumes Joseph is dead and treats Rachel’s remaining son Benjamin as the last fragile vestige of his beloved wife’s memory. The chapter is one of the OT’s most painfully ironic: the man Jacob refuses to lose Benjamin to is in fact Joseph, the very son Jacob assumes already lost. The Joseph cycle’s entire dramatic structure depends on this irony — the reader knows what Jacob does not, and the suspense across chs 42–45 is generated by the gap. The verse is also significant for what it implies about the dynamics of Jacob’s remaining family: the older sons (sons of Leah, Bilhah, Zilpah) feel themselves expendable in Jacob’s grief, replaceable, ungrieved-for; only Rachel’s sons — Joseph (presumed dead) and Benjamin — receive Jacob’s full attention. The favoritism that drove the brothers to violence in ch 37 has not been corrected.',
        "User original",
    ),
