        "",
        "I’ll be collateral for him",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Judah’s guarantee.</strong> Judah’s offer to personally guarantee Benjamin’s safety marks his moral transformation — the same Judah who proposed selling Joseph (37:26–27) now pledges himself for the youngest brother. The Hebrew root <em>ʿarav</em> (“to pledge, stand surety”) is the same root as Judah’s later great surety-speech at 44:18–34. The transformation becomes the chapter’s theological center: rebellion repented becomes responsibility offered. The pattern (a brother once willing to sell Joseph now pledging himself for Benjamin) is the moral arc Joseph has been waiting for.",
        "User original",
