        "May God Almighty give you mercy",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Jacob’s resignation.</strong> Jacob’s fatalistic “If I am bereaved of my children, I am bereaved” (closing the verse) parallels Esther 4:16 (“if I perish, I perish”). The language of resigned acceptance becomes a Hebrew-Bible motif of submission to providential plan even at apparent personal cost. Jacob’s invocation of <em>El Shaddai</em> (“God Almighty”) — the patriarchal divine name — is significant: at the moment of greatest fear, he reaches for the oldest covenant identification. The pairing of divine title with human resignation models prayer as both petition and surrender.",
