        32,
        "",
        "an abomination to the Egyptians",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Egyptian dietary separation.</strong> The text notes that Egyptians would not eat with Hebrews — an example of the rigid social-ethnic boundaries of ancient Egyptian culture. Egyptian sources confirm strong taboos around shared meals with foreigners, especially shepherds (the Hebrews’ primary occupation). The verse’s editorial interest is theological as much as ethnographic: Israel’s later separation laws (Exodus, Leviticus) will have analogues even within the Egyptian context. Acts 10–11 (Peter’s Cornelius vision) inverts the principle — the New Testament breaks down the table-fellowship boundary that Genesis 43:32 presupposes.",
        "User original",
