    (
        44,
        18,
        "",
        "Then Judah came near to him",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Judah’s plea.</strong> Judah’s extended speech (44:18–34) is the longest single speech in Genesis — the most sustained piece of rhetoric the book contains. The speech rehearses the family history from a brother’s perspective, pleads for Benjamin’s release, and offers Judah himself as substitute. The literary craftsmanship is extraordinary: emotional appeal, narrative summary, legal argument, sacrificial offer all woven together. The speech transforms Judah from the brother who once profited from selling Joseph (37:26) to one offering himself in another’s place — the type of substitutionary love later read Christologically in Christian tradition.",
        "User original",
    ),
