    (
        45,
        4,
        "",
        "I am Joseph, your brother",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>Joseph’s self-revelation.</strong> The recognition scene that resolves the cycle. Joseph’s words in 45:5–8 articulate one of the Bible’s major theological claims: "you meant evil against me, but God meant it for good" (50:20). The Joseph cycle (chs 37–50) is widely admired as the most novelistic narrative in the Pentateuch — sustained character development, dramatic irony, no overt theological statements until this revelation. The literary technique reaches its peak here.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        45,
        4,
        "a",
        "",
        "comm-ethiopian",
        "Tewahedo — Ephrem the Syrian",
        "Ephrem the Syrian (360).",
        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Ephrem the Syrian</strong> <em>Commentary on Genesis</em> <small>(c. 360 AD)</small><p>&#x27;And Joseph said unto his brethren, I am Joseph; doth my father yet live?&#x27; Ephrem reads the self-revelation as the model of merciful self-disclosure: the brothers, expecting wrath, hear MERCY first. Tewahedo theological-anthropology preserves this pattern: divine self-disclosure in Christ runs ahead of and overwhelms human expectation of judgment. The risen Christ&#x27;s &#x27;Peace be unto you&#x27; to the apostles who abandoned Him is the Joseph-pattern fulfilled.</p></aside>',
        "Ephrem the Syrian, Commentary on Genesis, Section XLV.4. NPNF Series 2, vol. 13, ed. J. Gwynn / Schaff (1898). PD.",
    ),
    (
        45,
        5,
        "",
        "God sent me before you to preserve life",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>God sent me.</strong> Joseph’s threefold statement that “God sent me” (45:5, 7, 8) gives the Joseph cycle its theological key. The brothers’ sale of Joseph is not undone or excused — but it is recontextualized within divine providence. The pattern of human evil employed for divine good becomes paradigmatic for biblical theology: the cross of Christ is the supreme instance (Acts 2:23, “delivered up by the determined plan and foreknowledge of God,” yet executed by lawless men). Joseph does not say his brothers did right; he says God overruled their wrong for life. The distinction is subtle but theologically crucial.",
        "User original",
    ),
