        "",
        "His heart fainted",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Jacob’s fainting.</strong> Jacob’s heart “grows numb” (Hebrew <em>vayafag libbo</em>) at the news that Joseph lives — too good to believe. The shocked-disbelief reaction parallels Luke 24:41 (the disciples disbelieving the resurrection “for joy”). The pattern teaches that great good news is harder to receive than bad news — the human heart, defended against further loss, struggles to receive resurrection. Jacob’s gradual transition from disbelief to acceptance (45:27, “the spirit of Jacob their father revived”) becomes a model for slow recognition of restoration.",
        "User original",
