        45,
        28,
        "",
        "I will go and see him before I die",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Israel’s resolve.</strong> Jacob’s transformation in this verse is signaled by his name — “Israel said” (not Jacob). The grief-defined patriarch becomes the resolved patriarch: he will go to Egypt to see Joseph before dying. The single line completes Jacob’s narrative arc — the trickster transformed by loss is now the patriarch ready to descend into Egypt for the family’s preservation. The descent into Egypt sets up the entire Exodus narrative: the family’s preservation comes through the very land that will become their later bondage. The mixture of preservation and pre-figured suffering becomes the dialectic of biblical exile.",
        "User original",
