        "Note",
        "<strong>Visions of the night.</strong> Jacob receives the last patriarchal night-vision (cf. 28:12, the Bethel ladder; 31:11, return-from-Laban dream; 32:24, Jabbok wrestling). The Hebrew <em>marʾot ha-laylah</em> (“visions of the night”) is rare phrasing — appearing here, in Job 4:13 and 33:15, and in Daniel’s dream-visions (Dan 2:19, 7:2). The phrase typically marks especially significant theophanic communications. The double-call (“Jacob, Jacob”) imitates earlier patriarchal calls (Abraham at 22:11, Moses at Ex 3:4, Samuel at 1 Sam 3:10) — formal address that signals high-stakes covenantal speech.",
