        46,
        3,
        "",
        "Don’t be afraid to go down into Egypt",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The descent to Egypt.</strong> Yahweh’s final patriarchal-period theophany — to Jacob at Beersheba, on the way down to Egypt — explicitly authorizes the descent that will be the precondition for the entire exodus narrative. The verse closes the patriarchal phase of the Genesis story: Abraham was called <em>up</em> to Canaan (12:1); Jacob is now sent <em>down</em> to Egypt (46:3). The chiastic geographical movement is theologically deliberate: the family must go down before it can come up as Israel. The promise Yahweh attaches ("there I will make of you a great nation") explicitly identifies Egypt as the place where the Abrahamic promise of nationhood will be fulfilled — Israel becomes a nation in Egyptian slavery, not in Canaanite freedom, theologically registering that nationhood is gift in spite of context, not consequence of context. The chapter’s detail of Jacob’s 70-person family (46:8–27) becomes the baseline against which Exodus 1:7 will register multiplication ("the children of Israel were fruitful and increased abundantly"). The descent that the patriarch fears is the actual mechanism of fulfillment.',
        "User original",
