    (
        46,
        29,
        "",
        "fell on his neck, and wept on his neck a good while",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The reunion at Goshen.</strong> Jacob and Joseph’s reunion — twenty-two years after Joseph was sold into slavery — is one of the OT’s most affecting scenes. The narrator’s vocabulary is precise and economical: Joseph "fell on his neck and wept on his neck a long while" (Hebrew <em>od</em>, "still, longer"). The detail of the prolonged weeping, after twenty-two years of bereavement, registers the depth of grief Jacob carried. Jacob’s reply — "now let me die, since I have seen your face, that you are still alive" (46:30) — frames his subsequent seventeen years in Egypt as bonus-time after the unexpected restoration. The chapter completes the structural inversion: Jacob, who once saw Joseph’s blood-soaked coat and concluded "a wild beast has devoured him" (37:33), now sees Joseph alive in Pharaoh’s second-chariot. The narrative arc of grief reversed by recognition — death assumed, life discovered — is one of the OT’s most influential narrative patterns, recurring in resurrection-narrative shapes across the canon. The verse is the formal closure of the Joseph cycle’s primary plot.',
        "User original",
    ),
