        47,
        29,
        "",
        "please put your hand under my thigh",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>Jacob’s burial-oath.</strong> Jacob’s oath to Joseph — by the same hand-under-thigh formula Abraham used (24:2) — secures burial in the patriarchal tomb. The oath is theological as much as funerary: Jacob refuses to be buried in Egypt because Egypt is not the promised land. The body’s geography matters; identity persists in burial-place. The oath’s fulfillment (50:13, Joseph carries his father’s body to Machpelah) becomes the model for Joseph’s own analogous oath about his bones (50:25). The corpse-promises maintain land-orientation across generations — the bodies wait for the eventual return.",
        "User original",
