    (
        48,
        16,
        "",
        "the angel who has redeemed me",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The redeeming angel.</strong> The “angel who has redeemed me from all harm” parallels but does not equate with the “God who has shepherded me” of the previous verse. The redeemer-angel may refer to the Jabbok wrestler (32:24), or be a distinct mediating figure. The Hebrew <em>goʾel</em> (“redeemer, kinsman-redeemer”) is a key Hebrew Bible category — invoked in Job 19:25 (“my Redeemer lives”), Ruth’s redeemer-figure, and ultimately in messianic theology. Jacob’s invocation of God-as-Redeemer is the first use of this vocabulary in Scripture, anchoring the redeemer-theology in patriarchal experience.",
        "User original",
    ),
