    (
        48,
        19,
        "",
        "his younger brother will be greater than he",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The reversed blessing.</strong> Jacob’s explicit affirmation that the younger Ephraim will surpass the older Manasseh — over Joseph’s objection (48:17–18) — is one of the clearest articulations of the elder-younger reversal that runs through Genesis. The historical fulfillment is unambiguous: Ephraim becomes the dominant northern tribe, sometimes even functioning as a synecdoche for the entire northern kingdom (cf. Hosea’s repeated "Ephraim" for the northern kingdom; Isa 7:9, 17). Manasseh, while territorially large (with both western and trans-Jordanian portions), never produces the political-religious significance of Ephraim. The principle the chapter encodes — younger-over-older as the recurring divine pattern — is theologically consistent with the rest of Genesis but stands in continuous tension with the actual ANE legal-cultural premium on primogeniture. The Genesis pattern is theologically deliberate counter-cultural: the deity does not honor the social hierarchy that the surrounding cultures take for granted, but operates by criteria of grace and choice rather than birth-order. Paul will eventually cite the pattern at Rom 9:10–13 to articulate the same principle theologically.',
        "User original",
    ),
    (
        48,
        20,
        "",
        "God make you as Ephraim and as Manasseh",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The blessing formula.</strong> Jacob’s words become the standardized Jewish blessing for sons — fathers traditionally bless their boys on Friday night with this formula (“May God make you as Ephraim and as Manasseh”). For daughters, the corresponding blessing invokes the four matriarchs. The verse thus crosses from narrative into liturgical practice — a Genesis blessing-text becoming centuries of Jewish home liturgy. The chosen typology is significant: not Abraham, Isaac, Jacob, but specifically Ephraim and Manasseh — sons born in exile yet remaining covenant-faithful. The model for Diaspora sons is sons who flourished outside the land while preserving covenant identity.",
        "User original",
    ),
