    (
        49,
        1,
        "",
        "in the days to come",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The last days.</strong> Jacob’s introduction — “what shall happen to you in the last days” (Hebrew <em>be-acharit ha-yamim</em>) — uses the formula that becomes prophetic and apocalyptic eschatology’s standard idiom (Isa 2:2, Mic 4:1, Hos 3:5; in the NT, Acts 2:17 “in the last days”). Genesis 49 is thus framed not as ordinary patriarchal blessing but as eschatological prophecy. The blessings on the twelve sons function as predictions about tribal destiny in covenant time. The prophetic dimension informs the chapter’s status as one of the oldest poetic strata in the Hebrew Bible — and one of the most theologically charged.",
        "User original",
    ),
