    (
        50,
        2,
        "",
        "commanded his servants, the physicians, to embalm his father",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        '<strong>The Egyptian embalming.</strong> Jacob receives full Egyptian embalming — a 40-day process (50:3) followed by 70 days of mourning. The detail is historically significant: the patriarchal family at this stage is sufficiently integrated into Egyptian elite society that Jacob receives the kind of mortuary treatment normally reserved for high-status Egyptians. Egyptian embalming required specialist priests with specific ritual-religious dimensions (the embalmers worked under priestly direction, with elaborate prayers to Anubis); the OT does not specify whether the embalmers’ ritual elements were performed for Jacob, but the basic procedure is unambiguous. The chapter is one of Genesis’s most syncretistically realistic about the Egyptian context — Joseph and his family operate within Egyptian institutional frameworks even while preserving Israelite distinctiveness. The 70 days of mourning approximates the Egyptian standard royal mourning period; Pharaoh himself accords Jacob royal-level recognition (50:7, the mourning party including "all the elders of the land of Egypt"). The verse is one of the OT’s clearest pictures of patriarchal-Egyptian interaction at its most elaborate institutional moment.',
        "User original",
    ),
