        7,
        "",
        "all the elders of the land of Egypt",
        "comm",
        "Note",
        "Note",
        "<strong>The royal funeral procession.</strong> Joseph’s funeral procession for Jacob includes Egyptian officials and is conducted with seventy days of mourning (50:3) plus seven additional days at the threshing floor of Atad (50:10). The seventy-day mourning matches Egyptian royal funerary period for kings (Herodotus 2.86–88). The narrative quietly elevates Jacob to royal-funeral honors — the patriarch buried not as ordinary man but with ceremony befitting Egyptian state functionary. The detail signals Joseph’s full integration into Egyptian power structure while preserving Israelite identity through the Canaan-burial requirement.",
        "User original",
